 The data warehouse then starts syncing your Postgres data. You can see details and progress in the [sources tab](https://app.posthog.com/data-management/sources).
 
+## Selecting columns to sync
+
+By default, Postgres imports and direct queries sync every column in a table. You can choose which source columns to include so that columns you don't need (for example, PII) never land in PostHog:
+
+- **New-source wizard:** On the **Select tables to import** step, click **Columns** on a table to pick the columns to sync.
+- **Source schemas tab:** On the source page, click **Columns** on a table row.
+- **Schema configuration page:** Open a table's configuration and use the **Columns** section. A summary line shows how many columns are being synced (for example, "Syncing 4 of 9 columns").
+
+Primary key columns and the active incremental field are always included, even if you don't select them. Disabled columns are hidden from the table's queryable schema, so they won't appear in HogQL queries. The underlying data isn't deleted, so re-enabling a column repopulates it on the next sync.
+
+If you add columns to a table that uses incremental, append-only, or CDC sync and has already synced data, PostHog asks whether to **sync forward only** (existing rows keep their previous values for the new columns) or **full resync now** (re-imports the table from scratch).
